constraint rather than a preference, so it is enforced mechanically as well as
stated here: `deny.toml` allowlists the licenses a dependency may carry and
`cargo deny check licenses` runs in CI on every pull request, transitive
dependencies included.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party components
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The following are consumed as declared dependencies. None is vendored into this
repository; the list records what a build pulls in and under what terms.

  risc0-zkvm, risc0-build (RISC Zero)              Apache-2.0
  lee_core (logos-execution-zone, LEZ)             MIT OR Apache-2.0
  k256, and the RustCrypto crates beneath it       Apache-2.0 OR MIT
  tiny-keccak                                      CC0-1.0

RISC Zero's accelerated forks of `k256`, `tiny-keccak`, `crypto-bigint` and
`sha2` are consumed through `[patch.crates-io]` in the guest manifests. Each
carries its upstream license, unchanged.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RedStone
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

RedStone's data-package wire format is decoded by first-party code in this
repository. Two boundaries are deliberate and are the reason this section exists:

  * The RedStone Rust SDK (Boost Software License 1.0) may be consumed as a
    declared dependency where it is guest-compatible. Boost is permissive and
    compatible with both licenses above. None of its code is vendored.

  * One test fixture from it is vendored:
    `verifier-core/tests/vectors/redstone-sdk-sample-payload.hex` is a
    byte-for-byte copy of `sample-data/payload.hex` from
    https://github.com/redstone-finance/rust-sdk at commit
    50cd703eb908d249d65f322192af1757e56afc97, under the same Boost licence. It is
    a payload RedStone serialised, which is what makes it worth having: it is the
    only envelope in the test suite this repository did not assemble.

  * RedStone's EVM connector is partly BUSL-1.1 and cannot ship in an
    open-source deliverable. No part of it is copied, ported or read for the
    purpose of writing the decoder here. Where byte-level conformance has to be
    checked it is used only as a black-box oracle against published test
    vectors.

`deny.toml` denies BUSL-1.1 outright, so the second boundary fails a build
rather than resting on reviewer discipline.

